    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-33926",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector",
      "problem": "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,0,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨2,0,1⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨1,0,3⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨2,0,1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ & =⟨1,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ & =5 \\\\ 2x+z & =5 \\\\ 2x+z-5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
