      "math_academy_id": "q-33165",
      "knowledge_point_id": "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line",
      "problem": "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(8,3,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\frac{x+2}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-2}{-1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨8,3,-1⟩.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨4,3,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨4,3,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ & =⟨8,3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ & =42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ & =42 \\\\ 4x+3y-z & =42 \\\\ 4x+3y-z-42 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
