    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-336111",
      "knowledge_point_id": "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line",
      "problem": "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,4,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=-1+2t \\\\ y=5+3t \\\\ z=7-t\\end{aligned}.$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,x\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$y\\,-\\,z\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨-3,4,1⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨2,3,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨2,3,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ & =⟨-3,4,1⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ & =5 \\\\ 2x+3y-z & =5 \\\\ 2x+3y-z-5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
