    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-128376",
      "knowledge_point_id": "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line",
      "problem": "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-5,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=3+t \\\\ y=t \\\\ z=7-t\\end{aligned}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨1,-5,2⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨1,1,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨1,1,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ & =⟨1,-5,2⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ & =-6 \\\\ x+y-z & =-6 \\\\ x+y-z+6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
