      "problem": "Given the Cartesian equation $9x-3y+6=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,,0$\n\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨$\n{{field-2}}\n,\n{{field-3}}\n$,0⟩=0$",
      "worked_solution": "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨9,-3,0⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-3y+6 & =0 \\\\ -3y & =-9x-6 \\\\ y & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$, we get $y=2.$ Hence, the point $P(0,2,0)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,2,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,2,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨9,-3,0⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
