    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-336220",
      "knowledge_point_id": "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation",
      "problem": "Given the Cartesian equation $3x+y+2z+4=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,0,$\n{{field-1}}\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨$ \n{{field-2}}\n$,1\\,,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩=0$",
      "worked_solution": "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨3,1,2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y+2z+4 & =0 \\\\ 2z & =-3x-y-4 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-2.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-2)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,-2⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-2⟩)\\cdot ⟨3,1,2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
