    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-326312",
      "knowledge_point_id": "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors",
      "problem": "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,4)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨1,-2,-5⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,1,-7⟩.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$y+z\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-3,2,4⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -7\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =19i+7j+k \\\\  & =⟨19,7,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =⟨-3,2,4⟩\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =-39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =-39 \\\\ 19x+7y+z & =-39 \\\\ 19x+7y+z+39 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
