      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors",
      "problem": "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-4,3)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨1,-3,-2⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,4,-1⟩.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,+\\,y\\,+$\n{{field-2}} \n$z\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨2,-4,3⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =11i+1j+4k \\\\  & =⟨11,1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =⟨2,-4,3⟩\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =30 \\\\ 11x+y+4z & =30 \\\\ 11x+y+4z-30 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
