    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-33142",
      "knowledge_point_id": "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors",
      "problem": "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(2,-5,1)$, $P(4,-2,3)$ and $Q(5,1,0).$",
      "worked_solution": "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨2,-5,1⟩,\\,p=⟨4,-2,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨5,1,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,3⟩-⟨2,-5,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨5,1,0⟩-⟨2,-5,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,6,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-15i+8j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-15,8,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =-67\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =-67 \\\\ -15x+8y+3z & =-67 \\\\ -15x+8y+3z+67 & =0 \\\\ 15x-8y-3z-67 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
